  "response": "**Main Subject:**  \nThe main subject of the excerpt is the societal expectation surrounding marriage, particularly for women of the upper-middle class in 18th-century England. It centers on the arrival of a wealthy single man, Mr. Bingley, in the neighborhood and the immediate concern of the Bennet family\u2014especially Mrs. Bennet\u2014about how this development might benefit her daughters through marriage.\n\n---\n\n**Summary:**  \nThe opening lines of *Pride and Prejudice* establish a central theme: the societal belief that a wealthy single man must marry, and that such a man is \"in want of a wife.\" This idea is immediately illustrated in the Bennet household when Mrs. Bennet excitedly announces that Mr. Bingley, a wealthy young man from the north, has leased Netherfield Park. She is eager to see how he might marry one of her daughters, especially since the family's social standing and future security depend on a suitable marriage. Mr. Bennet, though dismissive and sarcastic, is ultimately persuaded to go to the new neighbor, though he expresses little enthusiasm. The conversation reveals the tension between practicality and social pressure\u2014Mrs. Bennet's obsession with her daughters' prospects, Mr. Bennet's dry wit",
